#> tool: sedrepl
#> wait: 8 lemons 22
#> pause: 40
#> must: # command:  sed -n -f
#> must: 1 apples 12
#> must: 8 lemons 22
#> not: 2 bananas 30
#> source: 1p
# 1. The stream model. Sheet: "p" writes to the output stream; "-n" suppresses auto-print.
# sed reads a line into the pattern space, runs every command, then prints it,
# unless -n is given. With -n only what you p is printed. 1 and $ are line-number
# addresses: the first line of input and the last. The inventory is 8 lines.
# Try: remove the #ARGS line and watch lines 1 and 8 appear twice.
#ARGS -n
1p
$p

#DATA
1 apples 12
2 bananas 30
3 cherries 7
4 dates 16
5 figs 25
6 grapes 40
7 kiwis 18
8 lemons 22
